*Anonymity is a property of the whole record.* Information-management practice treats a record as data plus metadata plus context. Anonymity tools strip one piece of metadata, the network address, and leave the record itself untouched. Our results show that the remaining record still carries most of a personal profile, and that reading it no longer needs an expert: a free model, a published prompt and a few seconds per author are enough. For an anonymity-tool user, the network address is often not the most revealing thing they send.

*Why the rewrite falls short.* Reading the rewritten comments shows why a single pass leaks. The rewriter removes explicit statements ("I'm a nurse in Montreal") but keeps the experiences that imply them, because those experiences _are_ the meaning it was told to preserve. One rewritten author still "zone[s] into coding mode" and enjoys "crushing bugs"; another describes "grading marathons" in "the break room" and the responsibility of "teaching students"; a third complains about paychecks "feeling thin before loan payments". Each line is harmless alone, but together they let the model guess occupation, age and income. Meaning preservation and attribute removal pull in opposite directions, so a defense that keeps the conversation useful will rarely remove everything.

*Implications for tool builders.* Tools such as Tor-based VPN clients could offer an optional local step that warns users before posting, for example by running a small model over the draft and listing the attributes it can infer. Showing users what their own words reveal may protect them more than silently rewriting their text, and it keeps the user in control of what is published.
